      "Welcome to Swedish foreign policy and diplomacy. This is the single most dramatically reversed foreign-policy tradition covered anywhere in this course: military non-alignment defined Swedish identity for over two centuries, a defining national identity, not just a policy setting, until Russia's 2022 invasion of Ukraine triggered one of the most dramatic foreign-policy reversals in modern European history, ending with full NATO membership on March 7, 2024. Over the next forty minutes you will work through ten ideas in the order a real Swedish foreign-policy decision actually encounters them. We start with the end of non-alignment itself, the historical neutrality that preceded it, and the quiet Baltic remilitarization that began years before the actual 2022 reversal. We then turn to the specific, genuinely contested accession negotiation with Turkey that determined exactly how and when Sweden actually joined NATO, and to the close Nordic coordination that shaped Sweden's parallel path with Finland throughout. We turn next to the older, values-driven internationalist identity that predates and now sits alongside this new alliance membership, active UN engagement, a distinctive mediation tradition, and the feminist foreign policy framework Sweden pioneered and then discontinued. We close with two tensions in this identity worth holding onto directly: Sweden's own significant role as a global arms exporter sitting uneasily alongside its peace-oriented international image, and the settled, unaffected position Sweden holds inside the EU while still declining to adopt the euro. Keep one question running underneath everything you hear: is this a continuation of Sweden's older internationalist identity, or a genuine break from it forced by the 2022 shock.",
